---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'RV Battery: House vs Chassis, AGM vs Lithium';
const description = 'RVs run two battery systems — a starting battery for the engine and a deep-cycle house battery for lights and appliances. AGM vs lithium for the house bank, and how to choose.';
const canonical = '/lead-acid/rv-battery/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'RV battery',
  articleSection: 'Lead-Acid Battery Knowledge',
  dateModified: '2026-09-16',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What battery does an RV use?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'An RV uses two systems: a starting battery to crank the engine, and a deep-cycle house battery bank to power lights, fridge and appliances off-grid. The house bank is the one people mean by RV battery.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'AGM or lithium for an RV house battery?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'AGM is cheaper, drop-in and proven; lithium (LiFePO4) is lighter, uses more of its capacity and lasts far longer, but costs more and needs different charging. Lithium wins for serious off-grid use; AGM wins on upfront cost.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-16"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Applications · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>RV Battery: House vs Chassis, AGM vs Lithium</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        An RV actually carries two battery systems with opposite jobs — and most "RV battery" confusion comes from
        mixing them up.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> An RV uses <strong>two battery systems</strong>: a <strong>starting
        battery</strong> to crank the engine (a standard <a href="/lead-acid/sli-battery/">SLI</a> job), and a
        <strong>deep-cycle house battery bank</strong> to power lights, fridge and appliances off-grid. For the
        house bank the choice is <strong>AGM</strong> (cheaper, drop-in, proven) or <strong>lithium (LiFePO4)</strong>
        (lighter, deeper usable capacity, much longer life, higher cost).
      </div>

      <h2>The two systems, separated</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Chassis (starting)</th><th>House (deep-cycle)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Job</strong></td><td>Crank the engine</td><td>Run lights, fridge, appliances off-grid</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Battery</strong></td><td>SLI starting battery</td><td>Deep-cycle AGM or lithium</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Failure if wrong</strong></td><td>—</td><td>A starting battery deep-cycled dies in weeks</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The house bank is the one that earns the "RV battery" name, and it must be a
        <a href="/lead-acid/deep-cycle-vs-starting/">deep-cycle</a> design — the discharge is deep and daily,
        the opposite of a starting battery's duty.
      </p>

      <h2>AGM vs lithium for the house bank</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>AGM</th><th>LiFePO4</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Usable capacity</strong></td><td>~50% of rated</td><td>~80–90% of rated</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Weight</strong></td><td>Heavy</td><td>~half the weight</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Cycle life</strong></td><td>~500–800 deep cycles</td><td>~2,000–5,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Cost</strong></td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher up front</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Charging</strong></td><td>Standard chargers work</td><td>Needs lithium profile</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>Position:</strong> The RV battery decision is a usage-frequency decision — weekenders should buy
          AGM, full-timers and serious boondockers should buy LiFePO4 — and the mistake to avoid is putting either
          one in the wrong of the RV's two battery jobs.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Reasoning:</strong> Lithium's premium pays back only through deep, frequent cycling; occasional use
          never recovers it. Match the battery to the duty — deep-cycle in the house, starting on the chassis — and
          the two systems last as designed.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from battery engineering references and RV/marine power documentation.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery engineering references — deep-cycle AGM and LiFePO4 specifications.</li>
        <li>RV and marine power documentation — house-bank sizing and charging.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
